====================================================================
DOMAIN: Liberation Ethics versus Discourse Ethics
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-08
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 6, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The scholarship comparing Dussel's ethics of liberation with Habermasian and Apelian discourse ethics falls into a clearly defined debate that unfolded primarily between 1995 and 2021. The central philosophical conflict concerns the relationship between formal-procedural morality and material ethics: Habermas and Apel argue that normative validity is grounded in the symmetry conditions of ideal communicative discourse, whereas Dussel insists that any ethics which brackets the bodily, material needs of life -- and the figure of the excluded -- is ideologically compromised. Dussel's architectonic ethics deploys three structural moments (material, formal/discursive, and feasibility principles), critically appropriating Habermasian discourse ethics at the formal level while arguing it requires supplementation by a material criterion grounded in the reproduction of life and the "negativity of victims."

Key positions in the debate include: (1) Dussel's claim that the ideal speech situation silently excludes those who cannot participate -- the poor, the colonized, victims of the world system -- and thus functions as ideological legitimation of existing communicative communities; (2) Apel's response (in "Discourse Ethics before the Challenge of Liberation Philosophy") that liberation philosophy misunderstands discourse ethics by conflating ideal validity with strategic application, and that the Part B application of discourse ethics can accommodate material concerns; (3) Mendieta's mediating position that the two ethics are complementary rather than antagonistic, each covering different dimensions of moral theory; (4) Schelkshorn's comparative reconstruction showing how Dussel's material principle provides what discourse ethics lacks. Recent work (Allen and Mendieta 2021) has reopened the question by situating Dussel's critique within a broader decolonial critique of critical theory's normative foundations.

The debate was institutionalized in a series of exchanges published in Philosophy and Social Criticism (1995-1997) and Philosophy Today (1997), and has since been taken up by scholars working on Latin American political thought, decolonial theory, and the intersections of critical theory with global justice.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly constitutes the secondary literature on Dussel's engagement with Habermasian discourse ethics. The papers here examine the material/formal distinction as a structural critique (Dussel 1997), the methodological response from the discourse ethics side (Apel/Mendieta 1996), interpretive assessments of whether the positions are complementary or incompatible (Mendieta 1995, Schelkshorn 2018, Zuniga 2021), and broader contextualizations of Dussel's project within critical theory (Dallmayr 2004, Allen & Mendieta 2021).

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Hans Schelkshorn's earlier German-language work on the Apel-Dussel exchange (including his 1992 "Ethik der Befreiung") was not retrievable in English-language databases. The "Ética discursiva. Apel en diálogo con ética de la liberación" (Dussel, 2018, S2 ID 0919d728) is a Spanish-language text not verifiable through CrossRef. The specific content of Apel's contribution to the Alcoff-Mendieta 2000 volume could not be verified independently.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The papers naturally group into (a) foundational primary-secondary texts where Dussel himself articulates the critique (Dussel 1997a, 1997b, 2012), (b) discourse-ethics responses and mediations (Apel/Mendieta 1996, Mendieta 1995, 1999), and (c) secondary scholarly assessments (Schelkshorn 2018, Zuniga 2021, Mills 2018, Cobos 2015). Synthesis should track whether each paper reads the relationship as supplementary/complementary versus structurally incompatible.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Dussel's material-principle critique: 4 papers -- Dussel's architecture for why formal discourse ethics requires a material principle of life
- Discourse-ethics response and mediation: 3 papers -- Apel/Mendieta, Mendieta 1995, Schelkshorn 2018
- Secondary scholarly assessment: 5 papers -- Mills, Saenz, Dallmayr, Cobos, Zuniga
- Decolonial-critical theory synthesis: 2 papers -- Allen & Mendieta 2021, Dussel 2012
====================================================================
}

@article{dussel1997architectonic,
  author = {Dussel, Enrique},
  title = {The architectonic of the ethics of liberation},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {23},
  number = {3},
  pages = {1--35},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379702300301},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dussel presents the systematic architecture of the ethics of liberation as a three-moment structure: the material moment (grounded in the reproduction of human life as a universal criterion), the formal or intersubjective validity moment (which engages and critically appropriates Habermasian discourse ethics), and the feasibility moment (practical-strategic action). Dussel explicitly argues that discourse ethics supplies a necessary but insufficient formal criterion: the community of communication must be grounded in the material fact of living subjects, and must be extended to include those currently excluded from discourse.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational scholarly statement of Dussel's position in the material-versus-formal debate and constitutes the primary philosophical reference for secondary literature on the topic. It directly sets out why the principle of discourse ethics, taken alone, cannot justify norms for the poor and excluded -- those who cannot enter the ideal speech situation.

  POSITION: Dussel's claim that material life-reproduction is a necessary transcendental condition for discourse, against discourse ethics' bracketing of material content.
  },
  keywords = {material-ethics, liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, Dussel, Habermas, Apel, formal-morality, High},
  abstract = {This contribution is a critical and constructive engage ment with discourse ethics. First, it clarifies why discourse ethics has difficulties with the grounding and application of moral norms. Second, it turns to a positive appropriation of the formal and proce dural aspects of discourse ethics. The goal is the elaboration of an ethics that is able to incorporate the material aspects of goods and the formal dimension of ethical validity and consensuability. Every morality is the formal application of some substantive good. Every ethical perspective demands its evaluation in terms of its uni versability. In order to achieve this mediation, it is suggested that we must incorporate not only the historical dimension of moral systems, but also the role of critical consciousness and the negativity embod ied by those who are victims of the existing consensus. The essay con cludes with six points that need to be considered when formulating a material ethics that is universalizable and, most importantly, that can address the massive poverty and dehumanization of those excluded from the present community of communication.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{apel1996discourse,
  author = {Apel, Karl-Otto and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {{`Discourse ethics' before the challenge of `liberation philosophy'}},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1996},
  volume = {22},
  number = {2},
  pages = {1--25},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379602200201},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Apel's interview-based response to Dussel's critique distinguishes between Part A (the grounding of discourse ethics in transcendental pragmatics) and Part B (the application of discourse ethics to historical-material conditions). Apel argues that liberation philosophy misreads discourse ethics by treating the ideal speech situation as a sociological description of real communicative communities rather than a counterfactual normative standard; Part B of discourse ethics, he contends, can and must address the asymmetrical material conditions of global capitalism and the situation of excluded victims.

  RELEVANCE: This is the primary philosophical response from within the discourse ethics tradition to Dussel's challenge, and Mendieta's editorial framing contextualizes the exchange for English-speaking readers. It directly addresses the question of whether ideal speech situation symmetry is blind to exclusion, or whether it is a standard against which exclusion can be criticized.

  POSITION: Discourse ethics response: the ideal/real distinction vindicates discourse ethics against the charge that it legitimizes existing exclusions.
  },
  keywords = {discourse-ethics, Apel, liberation-ethics, ideal-speech-situation, exclusion, High},
  abstract = {Mesurant la contribution d'E. Dussel au dialogue concernant la relation entre l'ethique du discours et la philosophie de la liberation, l'A. confronte la conception d'une economie transcendantale, developpee par Dussel, a sa propre conception realiste des rapports conflictuels entre le Nord et le Sud},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mendieta1995discourse,
  author = {Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {Discourse ethics and liberation ethics},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1995},
  volume = {21},
  number = {4},
  pages = {111--126},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379502100406},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mendieta argues that discourse ethics (Habermas and Apel) and liberation ethics (Dussel) are not fundamentally opposed but rather address complementary aspects of a complete moral theory. Discourse ethics provides formal criteria for validity; liberation ethics supplies the material criterion of the dignity of life and the obligation to the Other who calls from outside the existing communication community. The two ethics need each other: discourse ethics lacks the material principle and the perspective of the excluded, while liberation ethics lacks formal universalizability criteria.

  RELEVANCE: This early mediating essay by the major translator and interpreter of both traditions offers a complementarity thesis that subsequent scholarship has had to either affirm or contest. It is foundational for mapping the scholarly positions.

  POSITION: Complementarity thesis: discourse ethics and liberation ethics as jointly necessary components of a complete moral theory.
  },
  keywords = {discourse-ethics, liberation-ethics, Mendieta, complementarity, Habermas, Dussel, High},
  abstract = {L'A. souligne la complementarite de l'ethique du discours de Apel et de l'ethique de la liberation de Dussel, en ce sens qu'ils redefinissent et rearticulent les frontieres entre les notions du soi, de societe (monde) et de transcendance (Dieu). Tous deux ouvrent la voie a une nouvelle theorie morale en insistant a leur maniere sur les frontieres du discours philosophique},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dussel1997principles,
  author = {Dussel, Enrique},
  title = {Principles, Mediations, and the {``}Good{''} as Synthesis (from {``}Discourse Ethics{''} to {``}Ethics of Liberation{''}},
  journal = {Philosophy Today},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {41},
  number = {Supplement},
  pages = {55--66},
  doi = {10.5840/philtoday199741supplement62},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dussel elaborates the movement from the formal moment of discourse ethics (intersubjective validity, the principle U of universalizability) to the material principle of life as constitutive of the ethics of liberation. He argues that the "good" as a synthesis involves mediations between formal validity and material content, and that ethics of liberation moves beyond discourse ethics precisely by inserting the material principle of the reproduction of life as the primary ethical criterion, making the community of victims -- those excluded from the dominant communication community -- the critical locus of ethics.

  RELEVANCE: This supplements and extends the architectonic paper (1997a) by tracing the philosophical movement from discourse ethics to liberation ethics as a progression requiring the addition of a material criterion. Essential for understanding how Dussel positions his work as building on rather than simply rejecting discourse ethics.

  POSITION: Transitional account of how liberation ethics incorporates and transcends formal discourse ethics.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, material-principle, Dussel, good-synthesis, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{mendieta1999review,
  author = {Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {Review essay: Ethics for an age of globalization and exclusion},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1999},
  volume = {25},
  number = {2},
  pages = {115--121},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379902500206},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mendieta's review essay assesses Dussel's Ethics of Liberation (the large Spanish-language work published 1998) by placing it within the context of the Apel-Dussel debate and broader debates about the grounds of moral theory under globalization. He argues that Dussel's work constitutes a major contribution to universal ethics precisely because it begins from the negativity of victims and the excluded, giving liberation ethics a structural advantage over purely procedural theories when confronting the asymmetries of the global order.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an authoritative secondary assessment of Dussel's response to the Apel challenge at the moment of its most systematic articulation, directly relevant to understanding how scholars assess the Dussel-Habermas/Apel relationship.

  POSITION: Favorable assessment of Dussel's material ethics over procedural discourse ethics for conditions of global exclusion.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, globalization, exclusion, Mendieta, Dussel, Medium},
  abstract = {Dussel's ethics begins with a consideration of the importance of history for ethics in general and for us, in particular, in an age of globaliz ation and exclusion. The first part of the work concerns foundational ethics, where he grounds three principles: a material principle, a formal or validity principle, and a feasibility principle. The second part deals with critical ethics, where he grounds three additional principles of ethics: a principle of the recognition of the corporeal dignity of co-subjects, the critical-discur sive principle, and the liberation principle. This ethics offers itself as a third way between neo-Kantianism and neo-Aristotelianism, and as an ethics that reflects out of the pressing problem of the growing impoverishment of 75 per cent of humans on the planet.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{schelkshorn2018discourse,
  author = {Schelkshorn, Hans},
  title = {Discourse and liberation: the claims and contexts of two types of universalistic ethics},
  booktitle = {A Matter of Discourse},
  editor = {Nascimento, Am{\'o}s},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {197--218},
  doi = {10.4324/9780429464683-9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schelkshorn undertakes a systematic comparison of discourse ethics and ethics of liberation as two distinct forms of universalistic moral theory. He argues that both traditions share a commitment to universalism but ground it differently: discourse ethics in the formal-pragmatic presuppositions of argumentation, liberation ethics in the material principle of the reproduction of life and the ethical primacy of the victim. Schelkshorn argues that the two ethics are not simply complementary but represent different theoretical and contextual starting points, with liberation ethics arising from and oriented toward the perspective of marginalized communities in the Global South.

  RELEVANCE: This chapter provides one of the most carefully reconstructed comparative analyses available in English, directly addressing the formal-material distinction and the contextual embeddedness of each position.

  POSITION: Contextual differentiation: discourse ethics and liberation ethics as responding to different historical and geopolitical situations, not merely formally complementary.
  },
  keywords = {discourse-ethics, liberation-ethics, Schelkshorn, universalism, formal-material, High},
  abstract = {Discourse ethics continues the European tradition of universal morality at a purified level; it integrates critically the two waves of Enlightenment European history has crossed. The basic principle of discourse ethics is related to the rational adjudication between conflicting interests and claims. Discourse ethics reconstructs and grounds the moral intuition of universal justice with reference to a consciousness of the fallibilistic status of scientific theories and the experience of cultural relativism. Discourse ethics responds to Enrique Dussel’s problem through its demand for practical discourses, where all concerned should solve consensually their conflicting claims and interests. Discourse ethics and the ethics of liberation share the intention to overcome monological universalization through real interactions. The material implications of discourse ethics are immediately obvious under totalitarian conditions. Discourse ethics and liberation ethics try to reconstruct the anticipations which are included in moral intuitions.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{zuniga2021ethics,
  author = {Z{\'u}{\~n}iga M., Jorge},
  title = {Ethics of Liberation and Discourse Ethics: On Grounding the Material Principle of Life},
  booktitle = {Decolonizing Ethics},
  editor = {Allen, Amy and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  publisher = {Penn State University Press},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {107--126},
  doi = {10.1515/9780271090320-007},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zuniga argues that Dussel's material principle of life -- the obligation to produce and reproduce the conditions for human life -- cannot be derived from discourse ethics and represents a genuinely distinct transcendental claim. He reconstructs Dussel's critique of Habermas and Apel by showing that the grounding of moral norms in the formal-pragmatic presuppositions of argumentation leaves open the question of the material content of what is argued for; liberation ethics closes this gap by anchoring ethics in the concrete bodily life of the victim.

  RELEVANCE: As a recent contribution to the Allen-Mendieta volume on decolonizing ethics, this chapter represents current scholarly consensus on the Dussel-Apel debate, directly addressing the material principle and its relationship to discourse ethics.

  POSITION: Incompatibility thesis on the grounding level: the material principle of life cannot be derived from discourse and represents a genuinely independent ethical foundation.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, material-principle, victim, Zuniga, decolonial, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{mills2018dussel,
  author = {Mills, Frederick B.},
  title = {Enrique {Dussel}'s Ethics of Liberation: An Introduction},
  publisher = {Springer},
  year = {2018},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-319-94550-7},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mills offers a systematic introduction to Dussel's ethics of liberation for readers in the Global North, covering the material principle, the formal validity principle, and the liberation principle. He gives particular attention to the role of the victim as the critical standpoint from which the ethics of liberation delivers its normative claims, and situates Dussel's critical engagement with discourse ethics as essential to understanding the architecture of liberation ethics. Mills argues that Dussel's ethics is urgently relevant to progressive political and economic theory.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most accessible secondary treatment of Dussel's engagement with Habermasian formalism in English, making it a useful orientation text for the research project.

  POSITION: Explanatory-expository account of liberation ethics as building on and transcending discourse ethics.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, Dussel, Mills, introduction, material-principle, victim, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{saenz2017life,
  author = {S{\'a}enz, Mario},
  title = {Life and Ethics: On {Dussel}'s Ethics of Liberation},
  journal = {The Journal of Religion},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {97},
  number = {2},
  pages = {244--258},
  doi = {10.1086/690480},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Saenz examines the role of life -- biological and social reproduction -- as the material foundation of Dussel's ethics of liberation, arguing that this life-centered materialism distinguishes Dussel sharply from both Kantian formalism and Habermasian proceduralism. He argues that Dussel's ethics is not merely a supplement to discourse ethics but a fundamental reconception of the conditions under which ethics is possible, grounded in the bodily reality of those rendered victims by the prevailing system.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a scholarly assessment of the material-life criterion as the central differentiating factor between liberation ethics and discourse ethics, contributing to the question of whether the positions are complementary or incompatible.

  POSITION: Dussel's life-materialism as constituting a genuinely different ethical orientation from Habermasian proceduralism.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, material-ethics, life, Dussel, Habermas, Saenz, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{dallmayr2004underside,
  author = {Dallmayr, Fred},
  title = {The Underside of Modernity: {Adorno}, {Heidegger}, and {Dussel}},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2004},
  volume = {11},
  number = {1},
  pages = {102--120},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1351-0487.2004.00365.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dallmayr places Dussel's philosophy of liberation in comparative dialogue with Adorno's negative dialectics and Heidegger's critique of modernity, arguing that all three think from the "underside" of Eurocentric reason. He argues that Dussel's engagement with Habermasian communicative reason is best understood as a critique of modernity's exclusionary underside: the communicative ideal occludes what Adorno called non-identity and what Dussel calls the exteriority of the Other.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Dussel's critique of Habermasian discourse ethics within broader Frankfurt School debates, providing a comparative framework that is directly relevant to understanding the relationship between liberation ethics and critical theory.

  POSITION: Positions Dussel's critique of Habermas as part of a broader tradition of critiquing Western modernity's exclusions from within and from without.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, Adorno, Heidegger, modernity, Dallmayr, critical-theory, Habermas, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{cobos2015latin,
  author = {Cobos, Felipe Curc{\'o}},
  title = {Latin American Political Thought as a Response to Discourse Ethics},
  journal = {Latin American Research Review},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {50},
  number = {4},
  pages = {69--87},
  doi = {10.1353/lar.2015.0052},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cobos examines how Latin American political thought -- including Dussel's ethics of liberation -- constitutes a distinctive response to the Habermasian and Apelian discourse ethics tradition. He argues that Latin American philosophers, drawing on the experience of colonial exclusion, developed a critique of discourse ethics' implicit Eurocentrism and its failure to account for the material conditions that prevent genuine communicative symmetry. The paper situates this response within Latin American intellectual history.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a scholarly contextualization of Dussel's engagement with discourse ethics within the broader intellectual history of Latin American political philosophy, relevant to understanding the stakes and reception of the debate.

  POSITION: Latin American political thought as offering a systematic and regionally grounded response to Eurocentric discourse ethics.
  },
  keywords = {Latin-American-philosophy, discourse-ethics, liberation-ethics, Cobos, Dussel, Habermas, Medium},
  abstract = {This article offers an articulation of liberation philosophy, a Latin American form of political and philosophical thought that is largely not followed in European and Anglo-American political circles. Liberation philosophy has posed serious challenges to Jürgen Habermas’s and Karl-Otto Apel’s discourse ethics. Here I explain what these challenges consist of and argue that Apel’s response to Latin American political thought shows that discourse ethics can maintain internal consistency only if it is subsumed under the program of liberation philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dussel2012critical,
  author = {Dussel, Enrique},
  title = {From Critical Theory to the Philosophy of Liberation: Some Themes for Dialogue},
  journal = {TRANSMODERNITY},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {1},
  number = {2},
  doi = {10.5070/t412011806},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dussel maps the relationship between the Frankfurt School tradition of critical theory (Habermas, Apel, Honneth) and the Latin American philosophy of liberation, identifying points of convergence and divergence. He argues that critical theory remains embedded in a Eurocentric horizon that, despite its emancipatory intent, fails to think from the perspective of the colonial other. The philosophy of liberation offers to enrich critical theory with a "South-South" perspective, while discourse ethics can contribute its formal validity criteria to liberation ethics.

  RELEVANCE: This is Dussel's own statement of the dialogue between critical theory and liberation philosophy, directly relevant to the question of whether the two traditions are complementary or fundamentally incompatible.

  POSITION: Dussel's own dialogical position: critical theory and liberation philosophy as engaged in a productive but asymmetric dialogue, with liberation ethics providing the material and geopolitical supplement.
  },
  keywords = {critical-theory, liberation-philosophy, Dussel, Habermas, Frankfurt-School, dialogue, Medium},
